.SH NAME
ktm-guide-inline-assembly \- Inline assembly (chapter 28 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
An \fBasm { }\fR block holds machine instructions, assembled by ktm itself for x86_64 or arm64. The routines it defines are called from ktm like any function. This chapter covers writing asm routines, binding them to ktm prototypes with \fB_extern\fR, how arguments and results travel, labels and loops, a function whose whole body is assembly, writing instructions as raw bytes, supporting both processors, and the errors.
.PP
Assembly is for the few places ktm can't reach: a special instruction, an exact sequence for performance, switching stacks. Everything else is clearer, safer and portable in ktm.
.SH A first routine
.PP
An \fBasm\fR block at the top level defines routines, each starting at a label followed by \fB::\fR\&. \fB_extern LABEL prototype;\fR gives one a ktm name and type, and from then on it is called like a function:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_ADD_ONE::
    MOV RAX, RDI
    INC RAX
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_ADD_ONE::
    ADD X0, X0, 1
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _ADD_ONE i64 addOne(i64 x);

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", addOne(41));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A routine runs exactly the instructions written: there is no prologue or epilogue, so it must end with \fBRET\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The block is assembled for the processor the program is compiled for, so code for each one goes under \fB#ifdef IS_X86_64\fR and \fB#ifdef IS_ARM_64\fR (see The preprocessor (see ktm-guide-preprocessor(7))).
.IP \(bu 2
Instructions and registers are written in upper case, Intel-style operand order on x86_64 (destination first), standard arm64 syntax on arm64.
.SH Arguments and results
.PP
Arguments arrive, and the result leaves, where the platform's C calling convention puts them:
.PP
.TS
allbox tab(	);
lb lb lb lb
l l l lx .
T{

T}	T{
Integer and pointer arguments
T}	T{
Float arguments
T}	T{
Result
T}
T{
x86_64
T}	T{
\fBRDI\fR, \fBRSI\fR, \fBRDX\fR, \fBRCX\fR, \fBR8\fR, \fBR9\fR
T}	T{
\fBXMM0\fR to \fBXMM7\fR
T}	T{
\fBRAX\fR, or \fBXMM0\fR for a float
T}
T{
arm64
T}	T{
\fBX0\fR to \fBX7\fR
T}	T{
\fBD0\fR to \fBD7\fR
T}	T{
\fBX0\fR, or \fBD0\fR for a float
T}
.TE
.PP
Two arguments, and a conditional move:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_MAX2::
    MOV RAX, RDI
    CMP RDI, RSI
    CMOVL RAX, RSI
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_MAX2::
    CMP X0, X1
    CSEL X0, X0, X1, GT
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _MAX2 i64 max2(i64 a, i64 b);

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d\en", max2(3, 9), max2(9, \-3));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
9 9
.EE
.RE
.PP
Floats go through the floating-point registers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_FMUL::
    MULSD XMM0, XMM1
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_FMUL::
    FMUL D0, D0, D1
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _FMUL f64 fmul(f64 a, f64 b);

i32 main() {
    printf("%.2f\en", fmul(1.5, 3.0));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4.50
.EE
.RE
.PP
A pointer argument is an address in a register; memory is read through it. Here the second field of a struct, 8 bytes in:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Pair {
    i64 first;
    i64 second;
};

#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_SECOND::
    MOV RAX, [RDI+8]
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_SECOND::
    LDR X0, [X0, 8]
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _SECOND i64 second(Pair *p);

i32 main() {
    Pair p = {3, 9};
    printf("%d\en", second(&p));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
9
.EE
.RE
.PP
The prototype is a promise: ktm checks calls against it, but not against what the instructions really do. A routine that reads a register the convention doesn't give it, or forgets to put the result where it belongs, returns garbage.
.SH Labels and loops
.PP
Inside a routine, \fB@@1:\fR, \fB@@2:\fR ... are local labels, for jumps within the routine; each routine has its own. A loop that adds \fBn + (n \- 1) + ... + 1\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_SUM_TO::
    XOR RAX, RAX
@@1:
    ADD RAX, RDI
    DEC RDI
    JNZ @@1
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_SUM_TO::
    MOV X1, 0
@@1:
    ADD X1, X1, X0
    SUB X0, X0, 1
    CBNZ X0, @@1
    MOV X0, X1
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _SUM_TO i64 sumTo(i64 n);

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d\en", sumTo(10), sumTo(100));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
55 5050
.EE
.RE
.PP
One block can define several routines, one after another; each \fBNAME::\fR starts the next.
.SH A whole function in assembly
.PP
A ktm function whose body is an \fBasm\fR block is a naked assembly function: its parameters arrive in the registers above, and its body is the instructions, with no code added around them. It needs no \fB_extern\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 addThree(i64 x) {
#ifdef IS_X86_64
    asm {
        MOV RAX, RDI
        ADD RAX, 3
        RET
    }
#endif
#ifdef IS_ARM_64
    asm {
        ADD X0, X0, 3
        RET
    }
#endif
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", addThree(4));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7
.EE
.RE
.PP
The block is the whole body: ktm code can't come before or after it, and the function must \fBRET\fR itself.
.SH Macros and constants
.PP
The block is read after the preprocessor, so \fB#define\fRd names, character constants and \fBsizeof(Type)\fR can be used as numbers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define STEP 5

struct Point { i64 x, y; };

#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_ADD_STEP::
    MOV RAX, RDI
    ADD RAX, STEP
    ADD RAX, sizeof(Point)
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_ADD_STEP::
    ADD X0, X0, STEP
    ADD X0, X0, sizeof(Point)
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _ADD_STEP i64 addStep(i64 x);

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", addStep(1));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
22
.EE
.RE
.SH Instructions as bytes
.PP
The assembler knows the common instructions: moves, arithmetic, logic, shifts, compares, jumps and conditional moves, calls, the SSE and x87 float instructions on x86_64, and loads, stores, branches, \fBCSEL\fR, barriers and float instructions on arm64. For anything else, \fBDU8\fR writes bytes straight into the routine, with a comment saying what they are:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_NEXT::
    DU8 0x48, 0x8D, 0x47, 0x01      // LEA RAX, [RDI+1]
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_NEXT::
    DU8 0x00, 0x04, 0x00, 0x91      // ADD X0, X0, 1
    RET
}
#endif

_extern _NEXT i64 next(i64 x);

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", next(77));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
78
.EE
.RE
.PP
An arm64 instruction is 4 bytes, written in little-endian order. Take the bytes from another assembler's listing (\fBobjdump \-d\fR). The library's \fBatomic\fR module writes its locked instructions this way.
.SH In a module
.PP
A module's \fBasm\fR blocks and \fB_extern\fR bindings belong to the module: a binding is the module's name, \fBpub\fR or private like any declaration (see Modules and packages (see ktm-guide-modules-and-packages(7))). This is how the standard library's \fBmem\fR, \fBmath\fR, \fBatomic\fR and \fBcoro\fR modules use assembly.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
module fastmath;

#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_FM_TWICE::
    MOV RAX, RDI
    ADD RAX, RDI
    RET
}
#endif

pub _extern _FM_TWICE i64 twice(i64 x);      // fastmath.twice for importers
.EE
.RE
.SH Errors
.PP
The assembler checks each instruction while the program is compiled, and reports an error at the instruction that has it (here line 4, \fBMOV RAX\fR, on x86_64):
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_X86_64
asm {
_BAD::
    MOV RAX
    RET
}
#endif

#ifdef IS_ARM_64
asm {
_BAD::
    MOV X0
    RET
}
#endif

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
needs 2 operands
.EE
.RE
.PP
A function containing an \fBasm\fR block that doesn't end in \fBRET\fR gets a warning: execution would run off its end.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBasm { NAME:: instructions }\fR at the top level defines routines; \fB_extern NAME prototype;\fR binds one to a ktm name. A function whose body is an \fBasm\fR block is itself the routine.
.IP \(bu 2
Routines get arguments and return results by the C calling convention, and must \fBRET\fR; nothing is added around them.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@@N:\fR labels are local to their routine.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#define\fRs, character constants and \fBsizeof(T)\fR work inside a block; \fBDU8\fR writes raw bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
Write a block per processor under \fB#ifdef IS_X86_64\fR / \fB#ifdef IS_ARM_64\fR\&.
